  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrently have a shortwave trough tracking across central/southern \nCA with upper ridging in place across the Plains. At the surface, \nhigh pressure stretches from the Appalachians into the Mid-Atlantic \nStates with lee troughing strengthening over eastern CO. \n\nJust like last night, low clouds are in place generally along and \nwest of I-135 and are expected to remain in place through at least \nthe late morning to early afternoon hours. Wichita will be on \nthe eastern edge of clouds and based on current satellite \ntrends, may clear out at some point this morning. Still looking \nfor gusty south winds this afternoon, especially for locations \nalong and west of Highway 14, where some gusts around or just \nover 40 mph will be possible. Opted to not run with a wind \nadvisory, mainly due to limited mixing due to cloud cover along \nwith the strongest winds being of short duration. Upper trough \nis expected to track over the Central Rockies tonight and out \nacross the Northern Plains on Wed. This will setup another windy\nday across the area for Wed afternoon with most of the area \nseeing gusts in the 35 to 40 mph range. \n\nPattern will remain very progressive with another shortwave trough \nset to track over Northern/Central CA by Thu afternoon and out \ninto the Great Basin Thu night. As this wave progresses east, \nlee troughing will again strengthen setting up another windy day\nfor Fri, especially across western and Central KS. \nUnfortunately it looks like this impulse will stay far enough \nnorth to limit precip chances on Sat. \n\nSlow warming trend will continue with all locations in the 60s by \nWed with upper 60s likely for both Thu and Fri. Cold front is \nanticipated to move through Sat night into Sun morning, knocking \nhighs back down into the 50s for Sun, which is close to normal highs \nfor this time of year.",
